    "q-109121": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109121",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=\\lambda (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -5)^{7},$ the nullity of $(A-5I)^{2}$ equals $1,$ and the nullity of $(A-5I)^{3}$ equals $2.$ If a basis of ${R}^{9}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5?$",
      "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{2}$ has dimension $1.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $1.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{3}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $1$ in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $2,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-5I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(A-5I)^{2}=2-1=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
