    "q-109747": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109747",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Suppose that a matrix $B$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{5}(\\lambda +3)^{2},$ the nullity of $(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ equals $2,$ and the nullity of $(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ equals $3.$ If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $B$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi ?$",
      "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $B$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(B-\\pi I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(B-\\pi I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(B-\\pi I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(B-\\pi I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $3,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(B-\\pi I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-\\pi I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(B-\\pi I)^{2}=3-2=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\pi .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
